   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( f(x) = 2 e^{- \\frac{x^{2}}{4}} \\) and \\( g(x) = - \\frac{3 x}{2} \\). Let R be the region bounded by the graphs of f and g and the vertical lines \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = \\frac{3}{2} \\). R is the base of a solid whose cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are semicircles whose diameters lie in the base. Using a calculator, find the volume of the solid. Show the setup for the calculation, and give the value correct to three decimal places.",
      "setup_required": true,
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "numeric",
      "mathjson": 4.727024629287953,
      "decimals": 3
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The diameter of each cross section is the vertical distance across R, \\( s = f(x) - g(x) = \\frac{3 x}{2} + 2 e^{- \\frac{x^{2}}{4}} \\).",
         "rule_named": "distance between the boundary curves"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Because a semicircle with diameter s has radius s/2 and area (1/2) pi (s/2)^2, each cross section has area \\( \\frac{\\pi}{8}s^{2} \\).",
         "rule_named": "area of the named shape"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The volume is \\( \\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}} \\frac{\\pi}{8}\\left(\\frac{3 x}{2} + 2 e^{- \\frac{x^{2}}{4}}\\right)^{2}\\,dx \\), which a calculator gives as about 4.727.",
         "mathjson": 4.727024629287953,
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99001",
         "rule_named": "volume by cross sections"
      }
   ],
